Ladybird lays out the columns of a table whose direction is rtl from
the right now, by mirroring the column indices of its cells and column
boxes once the grid is formed. Blink gets the same result by laying the
table out in logical coordinates under the table’s writing direction,
in TableLayoutAlgorithm::GenerateFragment(), and resolves collapsed
borders by that direction in TableBorders. WebKit mirrors each cell’s
inline position against the column positions in RenderTableSection::
logicalRectForWritingModeAndDirection() when the table’s writing mode is
inline-flipped. Gecko places cells in logical coordinates of the table’s
writing mode too, in nsTableRowFrame::ReflowChildren(). Chrome 158 and
Firefox 151 give exactly Ladybird’s cell positions now, and WebKit 26.5
differs only by a pixel in collapsed-border tables.
